<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iron Gates</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Đerdap, Porțile de Fier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iron Gates are the gorge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> where it cuts through the Carpathian and Balkan mountains, today forming part of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. For centuries a dangerous stretch for navigation and a strategic crossing point, the area was guarded by Roman and medieval fortresses. The Hungarian Banate of Severin was created around 1230 as a frontier march near the gorge, facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iron-gates.jpg" alt="Iron Gates" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cornelius Bechtler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danube_near_Iron_Gate_2006.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
